    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, quick question for you. We just bought a new CNC milling machine for the shop. It cost us $85,000, and we're estimating it'll have a useful life of 10 years, with a salvage value of $5,000 at the end of that. Can you walk me through how the straight-line depreciation calculation works for this so I can get it into the year-end reports correctly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To calculate straight-line depreciation, you'll use the following formula: Depreciation Expense = (Cost - Salvage Value) / Useful Life. Plugging in the numbers from your scenario: Depreciation Expense = ($85,000 - $5,000) / 10 = $8,000 per year. Divide this by 12 to get the monthly depreciation: $8,000 / 12 = approximately $667 per month. This amount will be expensed each year for 10 years and will be depreciated to $5,000 at the end of the 10th year.",
      "source": "target",
